    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d702">

                <pb facs="0699" n="687" sameAs="frus:frus1937v01/pg687"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">600.0031 World Program/197: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Belgium</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gibson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Brussels</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-08-23T17:00:00+01:00">August 23, 1937—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 23—12:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>68. My telegram No. 66, August 12, 10 a.m.<anchor n="25" xml:id="d702-f1"/> Frère
                    tells me that we need expect no further developments for several weeks. The
                    Prime Minister has come to the conclusion that it would be desirable to have
                    some form of agenda, however general, before undertaking the conversations
                    referred to in the draft note.<anchor n="26" xml:id="d702-f2"/> In his opinion
                    it is better to await the conclusion of the discussions which will take place in
                    Geneva in September concerning raw materials,<anchor n="27" xml:id="d702-f3"/>
                    etc., before trying to determine what should be embodied in this agenda. I,
                    therefore, doubt whether any further steps will be taken before the end of
                    September.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gibson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
